; within-cell comparisons\n  are reported for structure.\n- **Secondary claim (universal-flavored).** Any single benchmark/cell with raw\n  `Delta < 0` is reported as a per-benchmark inversion, but it is **subordinate**\n  to the statistical criterion: one inversion never by itself confirms the\n  research question; the main decision comes from the aggregate CI.\n\n## 2. Design overview and identification\nThe 70B-vs-13B gap is decomposed into three estimable components:\nsize effect (`alpha`), pre-training-adequacy effect (`beta * log(tok/param)`),\nalignment effect (`gamma0/gamma1`) plus a size x PT-deficit interaction (`delta`).\nA **2(size) x 2(PT adequacy) x 3(alignment)** factorial generates the cells:\n\n| cell                  | pre-training     | alignment |\n|-----------------------|------------------|-----------|\n| `adequate_rlhf`       | adequate (2T)    | SFT + RLHF (refined) |\n| `adequate_sft`        | adequate (2T)    | SFT only |\n| `adequate_base`       | adequate (2T)    | none (base) |\n| `insufficient_rlhf`   | insufficient (500B) | SFT + RLHF |\n| `insufficient_sft`    | insufficient (500B) | SFT only |\n| `insufficient_base`   | insufficient (500B) | none (base) |\n\nEach cell contains a 13B arm and a 70B arm.\n- **Within-cell comparison** `Delta_cell = 70B - 13B` (same regime on both sizes)\n  measures the pure size effect at a fixed regime.\n- **Adversarial cross-cell comparison** (the main H1 test):\n  `70B @ insufficient_base` vs `13B @ adequate_rlhf` \u2014 the larger model trained\n  worse than the smaller model trained well. If this inverts, \"insufficient\n  pre-training + lack of refined alignment negates size\" is demonstrated.\n\n**Identification trick that avoids most pre-training cost.** Llama-2 released\nbase AND chat checkpoints at 13B/70B trained on the same 2T tokens, so\nbase vs chat within a size isolates the alignment effect with identical weights,\nand size comparisons hold pre-training at 2T constant. Only the\n\"insufficient pre-training\" cells require actually training models.\n\n## 3. Operationalization (explicitly flagged: source vs assumption)\n- **Model size (fixed by task).** Official Llama-2-13B and Llama-2-70B\n  configurations (architecture family, tokenizer, 4096 context; 70B uses GQA).\n- **Pre-training adequacy.** Quantified as tokens-per-parameter (tok/param):\n  - *Adequate* = 2T tokens (matches the Llama-2 recipe; realized tok/param =\n    154 for 13B, ~28.6 for 70B). **Assumed source-adjacent fact** (to verify\n    against the primary source): 2T tokens for all Llama-2 sizes.\n  - *Insufficient* = 500B tokens for the 70B (~7.1 tok/param), well below the\n    Chinchilla-optimal ~20 tok/param (**design assumption, a deliberately\n    strong but not absurd deficiency**; the 13B keeps 2T for the adversarial\n    cell). A sensitivity cell trains both sizes at 1T to separate absolute-token\n    from tok/param effects.\n- **Alignment refinement.** Three levels: base (none), SFT-only, SFT + two-stage\n  RLHF (reward model + PPO), the Llama-2 recipe. **Assumed source-adjacent\n  facts** (to verify): ~27.5K human-written SFT examples; RLHF on ~1M human\n  preferences. \"Refined\" = full recipe; \"weak\" = base or SFT-only.\n- **Deliberately not varied (controls).** Data distribution (same corpus mix for\n  all controlled cells), optimizer/LR schedule, context length, tokenizer,\n  evaluation harness/prompts, decoding.\n\n## 4. Outcome suite (pre-registered)\n| group | measure | decoding |\n|-------|---------|----------|\n| Pre-training quality (alignment-free) | held-out token perplexity on a fixed held-out corpus | sliding window, greedy |\n| Knowledge | MMLU (5-shot) | greedy, fixed prompts |\n| Common-sense | HellaSwag (10-shot), WinoGrande (5-shot), ARC-c (25-shot) | greedy |\n| Reasoning/math | GSM8K (8-shot, maj@100) | temperature 0.7, fixed seeds |\n| Code | HumanEval (pass@1, 200 samples) | temperature 0.8, fixed seeds |\n| Alignment quality | MT-Bench-style + AlpacaEval-style win-rate vs a fixed reference (LLM-as-judge; judge fixed across all cells) | fixed template |\nPer-benchmark seed standard deviations feed the standardized gap and the bootstrap.\n\n## 5. Statistical analysis plan (implemented in `analysis.py`)\n1. Per cell, per benchmark: mean over seeds for each size; paired delta;\n   standardization by the 13B seed std (fallback `|mean13|`); sign-flip for\n   lower-is-better (perplexity) so *positive always means 70B better*.\n2. Bootstrap 95% CI (percentile, 5000 resamples over benchmarks/seeds) on the\n   aggregate standardized gap per cell **and** for the adversarial comparison.\n3. Pre-registered decision: `negated = (meanG <= 0 and CI_upper <= 0)`. Reported\n   per cell and for the adversarial comparison. Secondary: list every benchmark\n   with `Delta < 0`.\n4. Linear model (per benchmark, or pooled on accuracy-scale benchmarks; perplexity\n   reported separately): `score ~ alpha*size70 + beta*log(tok/param)\n   + gamma0*align_base + gamma1*align_sft + delta*(size70 AND insufficient)`.\n   - `alpha` = size effect (positive for both worlds in the sanity run);\n   - `beta` = marginal value of pre-training tokens (positive);\n   - `gamma0/gamma1` = regret of weak alignment (negative);\n   - `delta` = any extra size-deficit interaction.\n5. **Derived negation boundary** (an output, not an assumption): solve for the\n   tok/param at which the 70B ties the reference 13B:\n   `tpp70* = tpp13_ref * exp(-alpha/beta)` (alignment-equal case) and per\n   alignment level `tpp70*(a) = tpp13_ref * exp(-(alpha+gamma_a)/beta)`.\n   Uncertainty by refitting on bootstrap resamples. Interpretation rules:\n   - If `tpp70*` is far below any trainable budget (e.g., < 1 tok/param), the\n     fitted model says PT deficiency alone cannot realistically negate size.\n   - If `tpp70*` is inside a realistic budget (e.g., 3-10 tok/param) or the\n     alignment-only gap `gap_70b_base_vs_13b_rlhf` is already <= 0, the model\n     says the moderator *can* negate size within feasible training ranges.\n\n## 6. Feasibility and execution tiers\n- **Tier 0 (this workspace, done):** pure-Python scaffold + synthetic sanity\n  tests. No claims; validation only.\n- **Tier 1 (primary, external GPU, cheap-ish):** evaluation of released\n  Llama-2 base/chat checkpoints at 13B/70B (needs ~hundreds of A100-hours for the\n  sampling-based benchmarks like HumanEval/GSM8K). This alone yields the\n  within-cell cells `adequate_rlhf/base/sft` by pairing base with chat, and the\n  alignment-negation evidence (`70B base vs 13B chat`) with **zero pre-training**.\n- **Tier 2 (heavy, external cluster):** controlled pre-training of the\n  insufficient-PT cells.\n  - Required FLOPs: ~6*N*D. 70B @ 500B tokens ~ 2.1e23 FLOPs; 70B @ 2T ~\n    8.4e23; 13B @ 2T ~ 1.6e23; 13B @ 500B ~ 4e22.\n  - GPU-hours (A100, BF16, ~40% MFU assumed): ~0.4M-0.5M for 70B@500B, ~1.7M\n    for 70B@2T, ~0.3M for 13B@2T, ~0.08M for 13B@500B. Total full factorial\n    order of ~2.5M A100-hours (~285 A100-years) \u2014 **the dominant cost is the\n    adequate arm at 70B**, which Tier 1 already supplies from released weights.\n  - **Degradation strategy (adopted if cluster budget is limited):** pre-train\n    only the insufficient-PT arms (70B@500B + 13B@500B); obtain adequate arms\n    from released checkpoints; run a small-size pilot (1B/3B with the same\n    2x2x3 factorial, ~1-12k A100-hours) to validate the protocol and transfer\n    `beta/gamma` estimates; optionally substitute any publicly released\n    partially-pre-trained 70B-scale checkpoint as an explicit proxy, declared as\n    such in limitations.\n\n## 7. Source-supported vs assumed vs designed\n- **Fixed by the task (source-supported):** sizes 13B/70B; the two moderators;\n  design-only, no compute.\n- **Assumed (external knowledge; verify against the primary source before any\n  fix):** Llama-2 same-2T pre-training across sizes; base/chat checkpoints exist\n  on the same base; ~27.5K SFT examples and two-stage RLHF; Chinchilla ~20\n  tok/param anchor; published benchmark magnitudes (e.g., 70B-chat > 13B-chat).\n- **Design choices (explicitly mine, not in the materials):** 500B-token\n  \"insufficient\" budget (~7.1 tok/param); three alignment levels; the fixed\n  benchmark suite and decoding; the adversarial cell definition; the statistical\n  superiority criterion; the regression parameterization and boundary formula.\n\n## 8. Uncertainties and threats\n1. Exact \"insufficient\" token budget is a design anchor; the negation boundary is\n   reported as an estimate, but boundary estimates far outside the tested token\n   grid are extrapolations.\n2. Released base-vs-chat pairs isolate alignment only to the extent that the\n   chat pipeline differs solely by SFT/RLHF (they share base weights by\n   construction, but the full data recipe is not fully public).\n3. LLM-as-judge alignment metrics can confound size and alignment; judge fixed\n   across cells; human eval (small held-out set) optional.\n4. Perplexity can favor the over-trained 13B (154 vs 28.6 tok/param); the sign\n   flips are handled in the scaffold but the metric is reported separately, not\n   forced through the accuracy regression.\n5. No real results exist; all `report_*.md` are synthetic-scaffold outputs.\n\n## 9. What would answer the question\n- If the adversarial comparison shows `meanG <= 0` and `CI_upper <= 0` on real\n  data (negation), while the adequate/refined baseline shows 70B > 13B, the\n  design concludes: scale does not *necessarily* win; insufficient pre-training\n  plus weak alignment can negate it, with the derived boundary quantifying how\n  much deficiency suffices.\n- If the baseline and adversarial comparisons both stay positive with\n  `tpp70* < 1 tok/param`, the design concludes the moderators cannot realistically\n  negate size within trainable ranges (the \"negation boundary outside feasible\n  range\" case).\nMDEOF\necho \"EXPERIMENT_DESIGN.md written\"; wc -c /w